      "updated": 1778475158638,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "user svc:\n\nuser sends registration / signup request from client.\nrequest goes to API gateway, then LB routes it to user svc.\nuser svc is responsible for signup, login, authentication and storing user profile details.\nuser db is sharded by hash(email_id), so user lookup is fast and load is distributed across multiple DB\nshards.\nwe use consistent hashing so if new DB shard is added or removed, minimum user data movement is\nrequired.\nuser svc also stores user quota info (total, used, remaining storage).\n\n\n\n\nclient:\n\nwhen user drops a large file in Dropbox/GDrive client, client does not upload entire file in one\nrequest.\nlarge file can be 5GB / 15GB / more, so sending complete file through backend is not scalable.\nbefore starting upload, client interacts with local metadata index.\nlocal metadata index checks if this file is already uploaded earlier (using file hash / fingerprint).\nif same file already exists, upload can be skipped (instant upload / deduplication).\nif file is new or modified, upload process continues.\nlocal metadata index also keeps track of file state (uploading, completed, failed, etc).\n\n\n\n\n\nquota + validation (via backend / user svc):\n\nbefore uploading, upload manager calls backend to validate upload.\nbackend (file upload svc) checks user quota using user svc / user db.\nit validates:\n    total storage limit\n    used storage\n    remaining storage\nif user has enough remaining storage, upload is allowed.\nif file size is greater than remaining quota, upload request is blocked.\n\n\n\n\n\nchunker:\n\nonce upload is allowed, client passes file to chunker.\nchunker splits file into multiple small chunks based on chunk size returned by backend.\nchunk size can be dynamic (like 5MB, 10MB, etc).\nfor each chunk, chunker generates checksum/hash.\nthis helps backend to:\n    validate chunk integrity\n    support resumable uploads\n    avoid re-uploading duplicate chunks\n\n\n\n\n\nupload manager svc:\n\nafter chunks are created, upload manager svc takes responsibility of uploading chunks.\nupload manager does not send full file to backend.\ninstead it talks to backend for:\n    upload init\n    getting signed URLs\n    final commit\nfor each chunk, upload manager uploads directly to blob storage / S3 using signed URLs.\nthis avoids sending large data through API gateway and backend services.\nafter all chunks are uploaded, upload manager notifies backend to merge/commit the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHow upload is processed ?\n\n\n\nStep 1: upload init\n\nupload manager first calls file upload svc to initiate upload.\n\nPOST /api/v1/files/upload/init\nrequest contains:\n{\n  \"fileName\": \"vacation.mp4\",\n  \"fileSize\": 524288000,\n  \"parentFolderId\": \"root\"\n}\n\nfile upload svc checks user quota from user db.\n\nif user has enough storage, it returns:\n\n{\n  \"fileId\": \"file_789\",\n  \"uploadId\": \"upload_123\",\n  \"chunkSize\": \"5MB\",\n  \"existingChunks\": []\n}\n\nin case of network outage or disconnect, client on reconnecting to internet sends request for resuming\nupload. \nfor resume upload, client sends previous uploadId.\n\nthen backend returns already uploaded chunks:\n{\n  \"fileId\": \"file_789\",\n  \"uploadId\": \"upload_123\",\n  \"existingChunks\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n}\n\n\nStep 2: chunk creation\n\nafter getting chunkSize from backend, chunker splits file into small chunks.\nexample: if chunkSize = 5MB, file is divided into 5MB chunks.\nfor every chunk, chunker generates:\n    chunkId\n    chunkHash / checksum  (SHA256)\nchunkHash is used later to validate whether chunk uploaded correctly or not.\n\n\nStep 3: get signed URL\n\nupload manager asks file upload svc for signed URL for every chunk.\n\nAPI: POST /api/v1/files/upload/chunk-url\nrequest contains:\n{\n  \"uploadId\": \"upload_123\",\n  \"chunkId\": 1,\n  \"chunkHash\": \"abc123\"\n}\n\nfile upload svc validates uploadId and chunkId.\nthen it stores temporary upload metadata in Redis cluster (Redis is used because this data is\ntemporary and only needed during upload session)\nthen it generates signed URL from blob storage/S3 and returns it.\n\n\nStep 4: upload chunks\n\nupload manager uploads chunks directly to S3/blob storage using signed URLs.\nfile does not go through API gateway or backend.\nclient can upload multiple chunks in parallel (like 3–5 at a time), which improves performance.\n\nonce chunk is uploaded, s3 returns checksum and validate with what is saved in redis\nif it dont match it again ask client to upload that particular chunk only.\n\n\nStep 5: final commit\n\nafter all chunks are uploaded, upload manager calls:\n\nAPI: POST /api/v1/files/upload/commit\n\nthis tells backend that client has uploaded all chunks.\nnow backend starts validation/processing.\n\n\nStep 6: save metadata\nafter validation, file upload svc commits the file.\nfile upload svc calls file metadata svc.\nfile metadata svc stores final metadata in metadata DB\n\nafter metadata is saved, upload is complete.\n\n\nStep 7: publish sync event (event driven approach)\n\nafter successful metadata save, file metadata svc publishes event to Kafka.\nthis event is used to notify other systems that a new file/version is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nsync svc:\n\nsync svc consumes file upload/update event from Kafka.\nsync svc supports two ways of syncing:\n    1. push / fanout flow\n    2. pull / refresh flow\n\n\n\n\n\n\npush / fanout flow:\n\nwhen new file/version is uploaded, metadata svc publishes event to Kafka.\nsync svc consumes this event.\nsync svc checks who should get this update:\n    same user’s other devices\n    shared folder users\n    users with permission\n\nif those clients are online, sync svc pushes notification to them.\nnotification only says:\n    “new file/version is available”\nit does not send full file.\n\nclient sync engine receives this notification and then downloads metadata/file if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\npull / refresh flow:\n\npull flow happens when client opens app, reconnects internet, or manually refreshes.\nclient sync engine sends request to sync svc with local metadata/version info.\nsync svc compares client local metadata with metadata DB.\nif backend has newer version, sync svc returns missing/updated files.\nthen client downloads latest metadata and file/chunks using signed URL.\n\n\n\n\n\nclient sync engine:\n\nclient sync engine keeps local device in sync.\nit checks local metadata index to know:\n    which files exist locally\n    current file version\n    missing files\n    outdated files\nif local version is old or file is missing, client downloads latest data.\n\n\n\n\n\nwatcher (client side):\n\nclient has a watcher running locally.\nwatcher continuously monitors file system for changes:\n    file modified\n    new file created\n    file deleted\nwhen user edits a file, watcher detects change.\n\nchange detection:\nwatcher does not immediately upload full file.\nit informs client sync engine.\nsync engine compares:\n    old file version (local metadata index)\n    new file version\nfile is again split into chunks.\n\nchunk-level comparison:\nfor updated file:\n    new chunks are generated\n    hash is calculated for each chunk\nclient compares new chunk hashes with old chunk hashes.\nonly changed chunks are identified.\n\n\ninstead of uploading full file:\nonly modified chunks are sent through upload flow:\n    get signed URL\n    upload chunk\n    validate\n    commit\nunchanged chunks are reused.\n\nafter upload:\nfile metadata svc creates new version entry.\nexample:\n    version v1 → old\n    version v2 → updated\nmetadata DB stores:\n    fileId\n    versionId\n    chunk mapping\n\nthis is how file uploading is optimized\n\n\n\n\ndownload svc:\n\nwhen user wants to download/open a file, request goes to download svc.\ndownload svc first calls file metadata svc.\nfile metadata svc checks:\n    file exists\n    user has permission\n    latest version\n    chunk locations in S3/blob storage\n\nif user does not have permission, request is rejected.\nif user has permission, download svc generates signed download URLs.\nand user can use this signed download url to download the file.\n\n\n\nuser sends request → DNS resolves to nearest region → request goes to nearest API gateway → then\nflows into services\n\ndata is replicated across regions using events (Kafka / CDC), so system is eventually consistent",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "user svc:\n\nuser sends registration / signup request from client.\nrequest goes to API gateway, then LB routes it to user svc.\nuser svc is responsible for signup, login, authentication and storing user profile details.\nuser db is sharded by hash(email_id), so user lookup is fast and load is distributed across multiple DB shards.\nwe use consistent hashing so if new DB shard is added or removed, minimum user data movement is required.\nuser svc also stores user quota info (total, used, remaining storage).\n\n\n\n\nclient:\n\nwhen user drops a large file in Dropbox/GDrive client, client does not upload entire file in one request.\nlarge file can be 5GB / 15GB / more, so sending complete file through backend is not scalable.\nbefore starting upload, client interacts with local metadata index.\nlocal metadata index checks if this file is already uploaded earlier (using file hash / fingerprint).\nif same file already exists, upload can be skipped (instant upload / deduplication).\nif file is new or modified, upload process continues.\nlocal metadata index also keeps track of file state (uploading, completed, failed, etc).\n\n\n\n\n\nquota + validation (via backend / user svc):\n\nbefore uploading, upload manager calls backend to validate upload.\nbackend (file upload svc) checks user quota using user svc / user db.\nit validates:\n    total storage limit\n    used storage\n    remaining storage\nif user has enough remaining storage, upload is allowed.\nif file size is greater than remaining quota, upload request is blocked.\n\n\n\n\n\nchunker:\n\nonce upload is allowed, client passes file to chunker.\nchunker splits file into multiple small chunks based on chunk size returned by backend.\nchunk size can be dynamic (like 5MB, 10MB, etc).\nfor each chunk, chunker generates checksum/hash.\nthis helps backend to:\n    validate chunk integrity\n    support resumable uploads\n    avoid re-uploading duplicate chunks\n\n\n\n\n\nupload manager svc:\n\nafter chunks are created, upload manager svc takes responsibility of uploading chunks.\nupload manager does not send full file to backend.\ninstead it talks to backend for:\n    upload init\n    getting signed URLs\n    final commit\nfor each chunk, upload manager uploads directly to blob storage / S3 using signed URLs.\nthis avoids sending large data through API gateway and backend services.\nafter all chunks are uploaded, upload manager notifies backend to merge/commit the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHow upload is processed ?\n\n\n\nStep 1: upload init\n\nupload manager first calls file upload svc to initiate upload.\n\nPOST /api/v1/files/upload/init\nrequest contains:\n{\n  \"fileName\": \"vacation.mp4\",\n  \"fileSize\": 524288000,\n  \"parentFolderId\": \"root\"\n}\n\nfile upload svc checks user quota from user db.\n\nif user has enough storage, it returns:\n\n{\n  \"fileId\": \"file_789\",\n  \"uploadId\": \"upload_123\",\n  \"chunkSize\": \"5MB\",\n  \"existingChunks\": []\n}\n\nin case of network outage or disconnect, client on reconnecting to internet sends request for resuming upload. \nfor resume upload, client sends previous uploadId.\n\nthen backend returns already uploaded chunks:\n{\n  \"fileId\": \"file_789\",\n  \"uploadId\": \"upload_123\",\n  \"existingChunks\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n}\n\n\nStep 2: chunk creation\n\nafter getting chunkSize from backend, chunker splits file into small chunks.\nexample: if chunkSize = 5MB, file is divided into 5MB chunks.\nfor every chunk, chunker generates:\n    chunkId\n    chunkHash / checksum  (SHA256)\nchunkHash is used later to validate whether chunk uploaded correctly or not.\n\n\nStep 3: get signed URL\n\nupload manager asks file upload svc for signed URL for every chunk.\n\nAPI: POST /api/v1/files/upload/chunk-url\nrequest contains:\n{\n  \"uploadId\": \"upload_123\",\n  \"chunkId\": 1,\n  \"chunkHash\": \"abc123\"\n}\n\nfile upload svc validates uploadId and chunkId.\nthen it stores temporary upload metadata in Redis cluster (Redis is used because this data is temporary and only needed during upload session)\nthen it generates signed URL from blob storage/S3 and returns it.\n\n\nStep 4: upload chunks\n\nupload manager uploads chunks directly to S3/blob storage using signed URLs.\nfile does not go through API gateway or backend.\nclient can upload multiple chunks in parallel (like 3–5 at a time), which improves performance.\n\nonce chunk is uploaded, s3 returns checksum and validate with what is saved in redis\nif it dont match it again ask client to upload that particular chunk only.\n\n\nStep 5: final commit\n\nafter all chunks are uploaded, upload manager calls:\n\nAPI: POST /api/v1/files/upload/commit\n\nthis tells backend that client has uploaded all chunks.\nnow backend starts validation/processing.\n\n\nStep 6: save metadata\nafter validation, file upload svc commits the file.\nfile upload svc calls file metadata svc.\nfile metadata svc stores final metadata in metadata DB\n\nafter metadata is saved, upload is complete.\n\n\nStep 7: publish sync event (event driven approach)\n\nafter successful metadata save, file metadata svc publishes event to Kafka.\nthis event is used to notify other systems that a new file/version is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nsync svc:\n\nsync svc consumes file upload/update event from Kafka.\nsync svc supports two ways of syncing:\n    1. push / fanout flow\n    2. pull / refresh flow\n\n\n\n\n\n\npush / fanout flow:\n\nwhen new file/version is uploaded, metadata svc publishes event to Kafka.\nsync svc consumes this event.\nsync svc checks who should get this update:\n    same user’s other devices\n    shared folder users\n    users with permission\n\nif those clients are online, sync svc pushes notification to them.\nnotification only says:\n    “new file/version is available”\nit does not send full file.\n\nclient sync engine receives this notification and then downloads metadata/file if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\npull / refresh flow:\n\npull flow happens when client opens app, reconnects internet, or manually refreshes.\nclient sync engine sends request to sync svc with local metadata/version info.\nsync svc compares client local metadata with metadata DB.\nif backend has newer version, sync svc returns missing/updated files.\nthen client downloads latest metadata and file/chunks using signed URL.\n\n\n\n\n\nclient sync engine:\n\nclient sync engine keeps local device in sync.\nit checks local metadata index to know:\n    which files exist locally\n    current file version\n    missing files\n    outdated files\nif local version is old or file is missing, client downloads latest data.\n\n\n\n\n\nwatcher (client side):\n\nclient has a watcher running locally.\nwatcher continuously monitors file system for changes:\n    file modified\n    new file created\n    file deleted\nwhen user edits a file, watcher detects change.\n\nchange detection:\nwatcher does not immediately upload full file.\nit informs client sync engine.\nsync engine compares:\n    old file version (local metadata index)\n    new file version\nfile is again split into chunks.\n\nchunk-level comparison:\nfor updated file:\n    new chunks are generated\n    hash is calculated for each chunk\nclient compares new chunk hashes with old chunk hashes.\nonly changed chunks are identified.\n\n\ninstead of uploading full file:\nonly modified chunks are sent through upload flow:\n    get signed URL\n    upload chunk\n    validate\n    commit\nunchanged chunks are reused.\n\nafter upload:\nfile metadata svc creates new version entry.\nexample:\n    version v1 → old\n    version v2 → updated\nmetadata DB stores:\n    fileId\n    versionId\n    chunk mapping\n\nthis is how file uploading is optimized\n\n\n\n\ndownload svc:\n\nwhen user wants to download/open a file, request goes to download svc.\ndownload svc first calls file metadata svc.\nfile metadata svc checks:\n    file exists\n    user has permission\n    latest version\n    chunk locations in S3/blob storage\n\nif user does not have permission, request is rejected.\nif user has permission, download svc generates signed download URLs.\nand user can use this signed download url to download the file.\n\n\n\nuser sends request → DNS resolves to nearest region → request goes to nearest API gateway → then flows into services\n\ndata is replicated across regions using events (Kafka / CDC), so system is eventually consistent",
