    {
      "id": "2R9ZOyEcqlDZVGCzCWNpv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 12506.311110682298,
      "y": 41918.21404414221,
      "width": 1456.2342118507459,
      "height": 8330,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "g1e3v5KdMbl0hAkRaFwlw"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bSot9l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 83636677,
      "version": 939,
      "versionNonce": 1769860235,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1778475291346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "user svc:\n\nuser svc is responsible for:\n    user registration\n    login/logout\n    authentication\nuser requests first come to API gateway, then LB routes request to user svc instances.\nduring signup:\n    user svc validates email/password\n    password is hashed using bcrypt\n    user details are stored in user DB\nduring login:\n    user svc validates credentials\n    generates JWT access token + refresh token\n    token is returned to client\nJWT contains:\n    userId\n    subscription info\n    expiry\n    roles/permissions\nclient uses this JWT token while calling other services like:\n    video svc\n    watch svc\n    search svc\nother services validate JWT locally instead of calling user svc again and again.\nuser DB is sharded using hash(email_id).\nconsistent hashing is used to distribute load and minimize data movement during re-sharding.\n\n\n\n\n\nsubscription svc:\n\nonce user logs into platform, user has to buy a subscription before watching premium content.\nsubscription svc is responsible for:\n    * fetching available subscription plans\n    * managing subscriptions\n    * tracking subscription status and expiry\nsince subscription data is relational and consistency is important, we use MySQL.\n\n\npayment svc:\n\nonce user selects a subscription plan,\nrequest goes to payment svc.\npayment svc is responsible for:\n    * processing payment\n    * talking to third party payment gateway\n    * storing payment transaction details\npayment svc can integrate with gateways like:\n    * Stripe\n    * Razorpay\n    * PayPal\nsince payment systems require strong consistency, payment data is stored in MySQL.\nafter successful payment, payment svc publishes event to Kafka.\nconsumer svc updates user subscription status, and notification svc sends payment success notification to the user.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch svc:\n\nuser can search movies/shows using:\n    * title\n    * genre\n    * actor name\n    * keywords\nsearch svc is responsible for:\n    * handling search queries\n    * fetching matching videos\ninstead of querying DB directly, search svc uses Elasticsearch because it is optimized for fast full-text search.\nvideo metadata is synced from video DB to Elasticsearch using CDC pipeline.\nresults are returned in paginated form.\n\n\n\n\nUpload svc\n\nUpload svc handles video upload requests from content creators.\nWhen creator uploads a video, request first goes through API Gateway and Load Balancer to Upload svc.\nUpload svc first stores video metadata like: title, description, creatorId, format. inside Video Metadata DB.\nSince videos are very large, resumable upload is used instead of single request upload.\nUpload svc generates upload session id and provides resumable upload URL back to client.\nClient uploads video chunks using this session URL.\nUpload svc stores uploaded video chunks inside object storage like S3.\nOnce upload is completed, Upload svc publishes upload completed event into message queues.\nContent Processor workflow engine consumes this event and starts:\n    chunking\n    encoding\n    format conversion\n    quality conversion\n    CDN upload\nAfter processing completes, Notification svc sends upload completion notification to creator.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nContent Processor (Workflow Engine)\n\nOnce video upload is completed, Upload svc publishes an event into message queue.\nContent Processor consumes this event and starts video processing pipeline.\nThe workflow engine contains multiple services working step by step.\n\n\nContent Chunker svc\n\nVideos are usually very large in size.\nInstead of processing entire video at once, Content Chunker svc breaks video into smaller chunks.\nFor example: 1 minute video divided into 10 chunks each chunk of 6 seconds\nChunking helps in: \n    parallel processing\n    faster streaming\n    adaptive playback\n    easier retries\nAfter chunking, chunks are stored in object storage.\nChunker svc then publishes chunk events into message.\n\n\nContent Encoder svc\n\nDifferent devices and streaming protocols require videos in specific encoding standards.\nContent Encoder svc converts chunks into streaming compatible encodings like:\n    H.264\n    H.265\nEncoding compresses video efficiently while maintaining quality.\nThis reduces:\n    bandwidth usage\n    storage cost\n    buffering during playback\nEncoded chunks are stored back into object storage.\n\n\nFormat Converter svc\n\nDifferent devices support different video formats.\nFormat Converter svc converts encoded chunks into multiple formats like:\n    MP4\n    MOV\n    WEBM\nThis ensures compatibility across: mobile devices, laptops, browsers, smart TVs\nConverted chunks are again stored in object storage.\n\n\nQuality Converter svc\n\nUsers have different internet speeds.\nQuality Converter svc creates multiple resolutions for each chunk like:\n    240p\n    480p\n    720p\n    1080p\n    4K\nThis enables adaptive streaming.\nIf internet speed becomes slow, lower quality chunks are streamed automatically to avoid buffering.\n\n\nCDN Uploader svc\n\nOnce all processing is completed, CDN Uploader svc uploads processed chunks to CDN.\nCDN stores video chunks closer to users geographically.\nThis reduces:\n    latency\n    buffering\n    load on origin servers\nCDN uploader also stores:\n    chunk URLs\n    available formats\n    available resolutions\ninside Video Metadata DB.\nUsing this metadata, manifest file is generated for video streaming.\nClient later uses manifest file to fetch appropriate video chunks dynamically from CDN.\n\nAdaptive Streaming (HLS/DASH)\nPlatform uses streaming protocols like:\n    HLS               (manifest file: .m3u8)\n    DASH           (manifest file: .mpd)\nVideos are divided into small chunks and streamed dynamically based on network conditions.\nManifest file contains:\n    chunk locations\n    available qualities\n    available formats\nClient continuously requests appropriate chunks from CDN.\nIf internet speed decreases:\n    lower quality chunks are streamed\nIf internet speed improves:\n    higher quality chunks are streamed\nThis enables smooth adaptive streaming with minimal buffering.\n\n\nNotification svc\n\nAfter complete processing pipeline finishes successfully, workflow engine publishes completion event into\nMessage Queue.\nNotification svc consumes this event and sends notification to creator that:\n    upload completed\n    processing completed\n    video ready for streaming\n\n\n\n\n\nPlay svc\n\nAdaptive Streaming Flow\n\nWhen user clicks play button, client first fetches manifest file from Play svc.\nManifest file contains:\n    chunk locations\n    available qualities\nClient continuously monitors current internet speed.\nInitially if internet speed is slow client starts streaming lower quality chunks.\nFor example: 2 Mbps connection → 720p quality chunk\nIf network remains stable: next chunks will also be in same quality\nIf network speed increases: switch to higher quality chunks\nFor example: 10 Mbps connection → 4K quality chunk\nStreaming happens chunk by chunk dynamically.\nExample:\n    Segment 1 → streamed in 720p\n    Segment 2 → streamed in 720p\n    Segment 3 → streamed in 4K\nIf internet speed drops:\n    client automatically switches back to lower quality chunks\nThis adaptive switching is handled using:\n    HLS\n    MPEG-DASH\nBenefits:\n    minimal buffering\n    smooth playback\n    optimized bandwidth usage\n    better user experience",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "user svc:\n\nuser svc is responsible for:\n    user registration\n    login/logout\n    authentication\nuser requests first come to API gateway, then LB routes request to user svc instances.\nduring signup:\n    user svc validates email/password\n    password is hashed using bcrypt\n    user details are stored in user DB\nduring login:\n    user svc validates credentials\n    generates JWT access token + refresh token\n    token is returned to client\nJWT contains:\n    userId\n    subscription info\n    expiry\n    roles/permissions\nclient uses this JWT token while calling other services like:\n    video svc\n    watch svc\n    search svc\nother services validate JWT locally instead of calling user svc again and again.\nuser DB is sharded using hash(email_id).\nconsistent hashing is used to distribute load and minimize data movement during re-sharding.\n\n\n\n\n\nsubscription svc:\n\nonce user logs into platform, user has to buy a subscription before watching premium content.\nsubscription svc is responsible for:\n    * fetching available subscription plans\n    * managing subscriptions\n    * tracking subscription status and expiry\nsince subscription data is relational and consistency is important, we use MySQL.\n\n\npayment svc:\n\nonce user selects a subscription plan,\nrequest goes to payment svc.\npayment svc is responsible for:\n    * processing payment\n    * talking to third party payment gateway\n    * storing payment transaction details\npayment svc can integrate with gateways like:\n    * Stripe\n    * Razorpay\n    * PayPal\nsince payment systems require strong consistency, payment data is stored in MySQL.\nafter successful payment, payment svc publishes event to Kafka.\nconsumer svc updates user subscription status, and notification svc sends payment success notification to the user.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch svc:\n\nuser can search movies/shows using:\n    * title\n    * genre\n    * actor name\n    * keywords\nsearch svc is responsible for:\n    * handling search queries\n    * fetching matching videos\ninstead of querying DB directly, search svc uses Elasticsearch because it is optimized for fast full-text search.\nvideo metadata is synced from video DB to Elasticsearch using CDC pipeline.\nresults are returned in paginated form.\n\n\n\n\nUpload svc\n\nUpload svc handles video upload requests from content creators.\nWhen creator uploads a video, request first goes through API Gateway and Load Balancer to Upload svc.\nUpload svc first stores video metadata like: title, description, creatorId, format. inside Video Metadata DB.\nSince videos are very large, resumable upload is used instead of single request upload.\nUpload svc generates upload session id and provides resumable upload URL back to client.\nClient uploads video chunks using this session URL.\nUpload svc stores uploaded video chunks inside object storage like S3.\nOnce upload is completed, Upload svc publishes upload completed event into message queues.\nContent Processor workflow engine consumes this event and starts:\n    chunking\n    encoding\n    format conversion\n    quality conversion\n    CDN upload\nAfter processing completes, Notification svc sends upload completion notification to creator.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nContent Processor (Workflow Engine)\n\nOnce video upload is completed, Upload svc publishes an event into message queue.\nContent Processor consumes this event and starts video processing pipeline.\nThe workflow engine contains multiple services working step by step.\n\n\nContent Chunker svc\n\nVideos are usually very large in size.\nInstead of processing entire video at once, Content Chunker svc breaks video into smaller chunks.\nFor example: 1 minute video divided into 10 chunks each chunk of 6 seconds\nChunking helps in: \n    parallel processing\n    faster streaming\n    adaptive playback\n    easier retries\nAfter chunking, chunks are stored in object storage.\nChunker svc then publishes chunk events into message.\n\n\nContent Encoder svc\n\nDifferent devices and streaming protocols require videos in specific encoding standards.\nContent Encoder svc converts chunks into streaming compatible encodings like:\n    H.264\n    H.265\nEncoding compresses video efficiently while maintaining quality.\nThis reduces:\n    bandwidth usage\n    storage cost\n    buffering during playback\nEncoded chunks are stored back into object storage.\n\n\nFormat Converter svc\n\nDifferent devices support different video formats.\nFormat Converter svc converts encoded chunks into multiple formats like:\n    MP4\n    MOV\n    WEBM\nThis ensures compatibility across: mobile devices, laptops, browsers, smart TVs\nConverted chunks are again stored in object storage.\n\n\nQuality Converter svc\n\nUsers have different internet speeds.\nQuality Converter svc creates multiple resolutions for each chunk like:\n    240p\n    480p\n    720p\n    1080p\n    4K\nThis enables adaptive streaming.\nIf internet speed becomes slow, lower quality chunks are streamed automatically to avoid buffering.\n\n\nCDN Uploader svc\n\nOnce all processing is completed, CDN Uploader svc uploads processed chunks to CDN.\nCDN stores video chunks closer to users geographically.\nThis reduces:\n    latency\n    buffering\n    load on origin servers\nCDN uploader also stores:\n    chunk URLs\n    available formats\n    available resolutions\ninside Video Metadata DB.\nUsing this metadata, manifest file is generated for video streaming.\nClient later uses manifest file to fetch appropriate video chunks dynamically from CDN.\n\nAdaptive Streaming (HLS/DASH)\nPlatform uses streaming protocols like:\n    HLS               (manifest file: .m3u8)\n    DASH           (manifest file: .mpd)\nVideos are divided into small chunks and streamed dynamically based on network conditions.\nManifest file contains:\n    chunk locations\n    available qualities\n    available formats\nClient continuously requests appropriate chunks from CDN.\nIf internet speed decreases:\n    lower quality chunks are streamed\nIf internet speed improves:\n    higher quality chunks are streamed\nThis enables smooth adaptive streaming with minimal buffering.\n\n\nNotification svc\n\nAfter complete processing pipeline finishes successfully, workflow engine publishes completion event into Message Queue.\nNotification svc consumes this event and sends notification to creator that:\n    upload completed\n    processing completed\n    video ready for streaming\n\n\n\n\n\nPlay svc\n\nAdaptive Streaming Flow\n\nWhen user clicks play button, client first fetches manifest file from Play svc.\nManifest file contains:\n    chunk locations\n    available qualities\nClient continuously monitors current internet speed.\nInitially if internet speed is slow client starts streaming lower quality chunks.\nFor example: 2 Mbps connection → 720p quality chunk\nIf network remains stable: next chunks will also be in same quality\nIf network speed increases: switch to higher quality chunks\nFor example: 10 Mbps connection → 4K quality chunk\nStreaming happens chunk by chunk dynamically.\nExample:\n    Segment 1 → streamed in 720p\n    Segment 2 → streamed in 720p\n    Segment 3 → streamed in 4K\nIf internet speed drops:\n    client automatically switches back to lower quality chunks\nThis adaptive switching is handled using:\n    HLS\n    MPEG-DASH\nBenefits:\n    minimal buffering\n    smooth playback\n    optimized bandwidth usage\n    better user experience",
